HUB.talks = {
  "1-1": {"src": "talks/audio/1-1.mp3?v=74101e51", "dur": 238.76, "voice": "Rob", "paras": [["Hey, welcome in. Get comfortable, because today we're starting at the very beginning: what accounting actually is, and why it matters to you.", 0.0], ["Most people picture an accountant hunched over a spreadsheet, typing numbers into little boxes. And sure, there's some of that. But really, accounting is a language. It's a set of concepts and techniques for measuring and reporting financial information about an economic unit. Usually that unit is one business, and its money is kept separate from the personal money of its owners.", 10.52], ["So who's listening when a business talks in this language? A lot of people. Managers use it to run the place. Owners and investors want to know if their money is growing. Banks and other creditors want to know if they'll be paid back. Government agencies use it for taxes and regulation. Analysts compare companies, and employees wonder if their jobs, and their raises, are safe. Each one is asking a question about their own stake in the business.", 33.36], ["Now here's the key insight. Managers sit inside the company. They can ask for any report they want, in any format. Everyone else sits outside, and they only get what the company chooses to publish. That one difference splits accounting into two big branches.", 62.71], ["Financial accounting serves the outsiders. Because outsiders can't see the details, and they want to compare one company with another, everyone has to play by the same rules. Those rules are called generally accepted accounting principles, or GAAP. In the United States, the Financial Accounting Standards Board, the FASB, writes them. Internationally, the IASB issues International Financial Reporting Standards. The reports that come out are general purpose: they're not tailored to any one reader, they're meant to be neutral, relevant, and faithful to what really happened.", 78.27], ["Managerial accounting serves the insiders, and management sets the rules. If a manager thinks some development costs are really a long-term investment, they can leave them out of an internal profit report. That's their call. The reports should still be logical, but there's no rulebook they have to follow.", 113.67], ["Quick word on technology. The tedious bookkeeping, recording every transaction by hand, is now mostly done by software, and more and more by artificial intelligence. That frees accountants to do the interesting part: thinking, judging, and helping people make decisions.", 133.09], ["And judgment is a big deal, because accounting numbers are not as exact as they look. Here's an example. Lakeside Motors sells a car for thirty thousand dollars. The car cost them twenty-four thousand, and it comes with a three-year warranty. So how much profit did they make? Well, nobody will know the true warranty cost for three years. But nobody wants to wait three years for a profit number, because by then it's useless. So the dealer makes a reasonable estimate, say nine hundred dollars, and reports a profit of about five thousand one hundred. That's accounting: timely information built on careful estimates.", 150.5], ["One last idea. What's something worth? Many items, like land, are kept on the books at historical cost, the price actually paid. That number is reliable, because you can check the receipt. The alternative is fair value: what the item is worth today. That's more relevant, but it's an opinion, so it's less reliable. Standard setters keep moving the line between the two, a little more toward fair value over time.", 190.05], ["So, to wrap up. Accounting measures and reports a business's finances for lots of different users. Financial accounting follows GAAP for outsiders. Managerial accounting follows management's own rules for insiders. And behind every number is some judgment. Keep that in mind, and the rest of this course will make a lot more sense. See you in the next lesson.", 216.11]]},
  "1-2": {"src": "talks/audio/1-2.mp3?v=1b48d3cb", "dur": 214.39, "voice": "Rob", "paras": [["Last time we talked about what accounting is. Today, let's talk about who does it, and where.", 0.0], ["Saying you want to be an accountant is a lot like saying you want to be a doctor. There are specialties, and they're pretty different from each other. Accountants work in three broad places: public accounting, private accounting, and government.", 6.52], ["Let's start with public accounting. These are firms that serve the general public. A business, a nonprofit, or just a regular person hires an accounting firm the same way they'd hire a law firm. Public accountants do three main kinds of work.", 20.72], ["First, auditing. An auditor examines the transactions and systems behind a company's financial reports, and then gives an independent opinion on whether those statements are presented fairly. Think of it like a home inspection before you buy a house. You don't just trust the seller; you want someone independent to look.", 37.22], ["Second, tax. Preparing and filing returns, sure, but also advising clients before they make a decision, so they understand the tax consequences ahead of time.", 56.18], ["Third, consulting. That can be almost anything, from designing an information system to studying how a company makes its products.", 68.57], ["Public accounting usually requires a license. In the United States, each state licenses Certified Public Accountants, or CPAs. Other countries have similar titles, like Chartered Accountant. The license tells the public: this person met real standards for education, exams, and experience, and they've agreed to follow a code of ethics.", 76.41], ["Next, private accounting, sometimes called industry accounting. These accountants work for one employer, a business big or small, or a not-for-profit like a hospital, university, or charity. They figure out what things cost, build budgets, analyze investments, handle taxes and finance. Some are internal auditors. And here's a distinction worth remembering. An internal auditor is an employee looking inward, checking their own company's controls, protecting its resources. An external auditor is an independent outsider, reporting to the public. Same word, \"auditor,\" very different seat at the table.", 98.98], ["Then there's government. Every level, local, state, and national, needs accountants. At the federal level you'll find them at the IRS, collecting taxes and examining returns. At the GAO, the watchdog that audits how federal money is spent. At the SEC, which oversees financial reporting by public companies. And even at the FBI, where forensic accountants follow the money in fraud cases.", 136.34], ["Now, the most important part of today. Ethics. Investors and lenders make big decisions based on financial statements. If those statements aren't honest, the whole system falls apart. So the profession builds in several safeguards. There's a code of ethics every licensed accountant must follow. There's the independent audit. There's government oversight, like the SEC. And, there's the ever-vigilant lawyer, because people who are hurt by bad reporting can sue.", 160.91], ["So here's the takeaway. Accountants work in public firms, private companies, and government. CPAs are licensed to serve the public, auditors give independent opinions, and through all of it, integrity is the whole job. People trust accountants with their money. That trust has to be earned every single day. See you next time.", 194.04]]},
  "1-3": {"src": "talks/audio/1-3.mp3?v=8c6ecfa5", "dur": 201.53, "voice": "Rob", "paras": [["Today we're looking at the single most important idea in this entire course. One equation. Every accounting system in the world, from a lemonade stand to a giant corporation, is built on it.", 0.0], ["It goes back more than five hundred years, to a Renaissance monk named Luca Pacioli. He came up with a way for merchants to track whether their trading ventures were working. And his method is still the foundation of the most sophisticated software today.", 12.53], ["Here's the idea. A business is a collection of stuff it owns, plus the claims against that stuff. So: Assets equal Liabilities plus Owners' Equity.", 26.8], ["Let's unpack those three words. Assets are the resources a business controls, things expected to bring future benefits. Cash, inventory, equipment, land. Liabilities are what the business owes to others: loans, bills from suppliers, any existing obligation to pay or to do something. And owners' equity is what's left over for the owners.", 37.74], ["Because it's an equation, you can rearrange it. Assets minus Liabilities equals Owners' Equity. In plain English: if the business paid off every creditor, whatever remains belongs to the owners. That's why equity is also called net assets.", 60.01], ["Think of a house. Say it's worth three hundred thousand, and you owe two hundred thousand on the mortgage. Your equity is one hundred thousand. Same logic, exactly.", 74.94], ["Now, who the \"owners\" are depends on the type of business. A sole proprietorship has one owner, so one capital account. A partnership has one capital account for each partner. A corporation is owned by stockholders, and its equity comes in two pieces. Capital stock is what stockholders put in when they bought shares. Retained earnings are profits the company earned and kept, instead of paying them out.", 84.38], ["Retained earnings go up when the company earns net income. They go down with net losses, and with dividends. Dividends are payments to shareholders, a return on their investment. Once you pay earnings out, they're no longer retained. Makes sense, right?", 103.53], ["Now let's put the equation on paper. That's the balance sheet, also called the statement of financial position. It lists the assets, liabilities, and equity at one moment in time, like a snapshot. Take Edelweiss Corporation. It has assets of eight hundred ninety-five thousand dollars. It owes creditors one hundred seventy-five thousand. So stockholders' equity is seven hundred twenty thousand. And that equity came from two places: one hundred twenty thousand that stockholders invested, and six hundred thousand the company earned and kept over the years.", 124.89], ["Here's a trap, though. Is Edelweiss worth seven hundred twenty thousand dollars? No! Lots of assets are recorded at what they cost, not what they're worth today. Their land cost a hundred twenty-five thousand, but it could be worth much more now. And some valuable things aren't on the balance sheet at all, like a strong brand or a trade secret. Some risks, like a pending lawsuit, may not show up either.", 158.24], ["So, the takeaway. Assets equal liabilities plus owners' equity, always. Equity is what's left for the owners. And the balance sheet shows that equation at a single point in time. Just remember, it's not a price tag for the whole business. Nice work today.", 184.84]]},
  "1-4": {"src": "talks/audio/1-4.mp3?v=ccb4a19e", "dur": 216.58, "voice": "Rob", "paras": [["Last time we learned the big equation: assets equal liabilities plus owners' equity. Today we're going to put it to work, and watch what happens when a business actually does something.", 0.0], ["Every time a business does something with money, we call it a transaction. Buying, selling, borrowing, paying. And each transaction changes the numbers. But here's the beautiful part. If it's recorded properly, a transaction never breaks the equation. Ever.", 11.63], ["There are really only two ways that happens. Either one asset goes up and another goes down by the same amount, so the totals don't move. Or both sides of the equation go up, or both go down, together.", 28.45], ["Let's watch it with Edelweiss Corporation, the company from last time. They start with twenty-five thousand dollars of cash and fifty thousand of accounts receivable. That's money customers owe them.", 42.51], ["Transaction A. A customer pays ten thousand dollars that they already owed. Cash goes up ten thousand. Accounts receivable goes down ten thousand. Notice, this is not a new sale. The company just traded one asset, an I-O-U, for another asset, cash. Total assets? Unchanged.", 53.47], ["Transaction B. Edelweiss buys thirty thousand dollars of equipment and borrows the money to pay for it. Equipment goes up thirty thousand. Loans payable, a liability, also goes up thirty thousand. Both sides rise together, so the equation still balances.", 73.84], ["Now, Transactions C and D are where students usually get tripped up, so let's slow down.", 90.33], ["Transaction C. Edelweiss does five thousand dollars of work for a customer, who promises to pay later. Accounts receivable goes up five thousand. Easy. But what's the other side? Here's the logic. Doing work for customers is revenue. Revenue increases income. And income, when it's kept in the business, becomes retained earnings, which is part of equity. So retained earnings goes up five thousand. Assets up five thousand, equity up five thousand. Balanced.", 96.07], ["Transaction D. Edelweiss pays three thousand dollars of expenses, maybe rent or utilities. Cash goes down three thousand. And expenses reduce income, which reduces retained earnings. So equity goes down three thousand too. Still balanced.", 126.22], ["See the pattern? Revenue pushes retained earnings up. Expenses pull it down.", 142.7], ["Let's define those carefully. Revenue is the benefit a business gets from providing goods or services to customers. Expenses are the costs of doing business, the outflows it takes to produce those goods and services.", 148.16], ["And one more thing. In everyday conversation, people say \"revenue\" and \"income\" like they mean the same thing. In accounting, they don't. Revenue is the total amount generated by the business. It's the top line of the income statement. Some countries call it turnover. Income is what's left after you subtract all the expenses. That's the bottom line. So: revenues minus expenses equals income.", 160.92], ["A coffee shop that sells a million dollars of coffee has a million in revenue. But if it spent nine hundred fifty thousand running the place, its income is only fifty thousand. Big difference.", 187.32], ["So, to recap. Every transaction changes at least two things, and the equation always stays in balance. Revenue raises retained earnings, expenses lower it. And revenue is the top line, income is the bottom line. You've got this. See you next lesson.", 200.66]]},
  "1-5": {"src": "talks/audio/1-5.mp3?v=7923d1ac", "dur": 215.33, "voice": "Rob", "paras": [["Imagine someone offers you a chance to buy stock in a company. What would you want to know first? Is it profitable? Does it have cash? Does it owe a lot of money? Those are the questions financial statements are built to answer.", 0.0], ["Some questions can't be answered with numbers, of course. Is management any good? Is a competitor about to crush them? For that, investors also read the notes to the statements, company websites, regulatory filings, and the financial press. But don't expect special private tips. Securities laws strictly limit companies from giving inside information to just one investor.", 13.81], ["Financial accounting talks through four standard reports. Let's walk through them using Quartz Corporation.", 37.42], ["First, the income statement. It answers: how much did we earn? Quartz had revenues of six hundred sixty thousand dollars and expenses of five hundred eighty thousand, so net income was eighty thousand. Notice its heading says \"For the Year Ended December thirty-first.\" It covers a period of time.", 45.78], ["Second, the statement of retained earnings. It's a bridge. Quartz started the year with two hundred twenty thousand of retained earnings. Add the eighty thousand of net income. Subtract thirty thousand of dividends. And it ends the year with two hundred seventy thousand.", 63.02], ["Third, the balance sheet. This one's a snapshot, at one moment: December thirty-first. Quartz has eight hundred thousand of assets, two hundred eighty thousand of liabilities, and five hundred twenty thousand of stockholders' equity. And look inside that equity. There's the two hundred seventy thousand of retained earnings, carried right over from the second statement.", 81.19], ["Fourth, the statement of cash flows. It explains why cash changed, in three groups. Operating activities, the day-to-day business, brought in thirty-five thousand. Investing activities used sixty thousand, to buy equipment. Financing activities brought in twenty thousand: a fifty thousand dollar loan, minus thirty thousand paid out as dividends. Net result, cash dropped five thousand, from sixty-five thousand down to sixty thousand.", 107.15], ["Wait a second, though. Quartz earned eighty thousand, but operations only brought in thirty-five thousand of cash. How? Because income and cash don't always arrive at the same time. If you do work on credit, you've earned the income, but the cash hasn't come in yet. Over time, those differences tend to even out.", 129.72], ["Two more traps. Dividends are not an expense. They're a distribution of income to the owners, so they show up on the retained earnings statement, not the income statement. And borrowing money brings in cash, but it isn't income. It's financing.", 153.2], ["Now here's my favorite part. These statements articulate, which means they lock together. Net income from the income statement flows into retained earnings. Ending retained earnings flows onto the balance sheet. And when you drop it in, the balance sheet balances. Exactly. It feels almost like magic, and it's why Pacioli's five-hundred-year-old system is still with us.", 170.27], ["So, the takeaways. Four statements: income statement, retained earnings, balance sheet, and cash flows. The balance sheet is a moment in time; the others cover a period. Cash is not the same as income. And the statements all fit together like puzzle pieces. That wraps up chapter one. Really nice work.", 195.05]]},
  "2-1": {"src": "talks/audio/2-1.mp3?v=fafccbc2", "dur": 235.55, "voice": "Rob", "paras": [["Welcome to chapter two. Today we learn the actual language accountants write in: debits and credits. Stick with me, because once this clicks, everything else gets easier.", 0.0], ["First, why do businesses need a system at all? In chapter one, we updated the balance sheet after each transaction. Picture a real company doing that on a giant whiteboard, erasing and rewriting all day. Chaos. And afterward, no one could explain where the numbers came from. So every business needs a reliable accounting system, one that processes transactions into reports and keeps a record of every single one.", 11.59], ["The basic building block is the account. An account is a record for one item: one for Cash, one for Inventory, one for each kind of revenue and expense. All the accounts together are called the general ledger. Picture a notebook with a separate page for every account.", 38.75], ["Now, instead of saying an account went \"up\" or \"down,\" accountants say it was debited or credited. Debit simply means the left side. Credit means the right side. That's it. Left and right. Not good or bad.", 59.31], ["Why bother? Because of one powerful rule: in every correct entry, total debits equal total credits. So if they don't match, you know immediately that something's wrong. With plain pluses and minuses, a mistake can slip right by. Computer systems won't even accept an entry that doesn't balance.", 73.91], ["So which accounts go up with a debit, and which go up with a credit? Here's a memory trick. Two words: DEAD and RELIC.", 93.81], ["DEAD. Debits increase Expenses, Assets, and Dividends. D, E, A, D.", 102.86], ["RELIC. Revenues, Equity, and Liabilities Increase with Credits. R, E, L, I, C.", 106.05], ["Say them a few times. DEAD, RELIC. Honestly, it's like learning vocabulary in a new language. Memorize it first, and understanding follows fast.", 112.48], ["Let's try one. A customer pays ten thousand dollars they owed you. Which accounts? Cash and Accounts Receivable. Cash, an asset, goes up, so debit Cash. Accounts Receivable, also an asset, goes down, so credit it. Ten thousand on each side. Balanced.", 122.96], ["That's the two-step routine for every transaction. One: which accounts are affected, and are they going up or down? Two: apply the rules. What triggers the analysis? Usually a source document: a check, an invoice, a time card, a bank deposit slip. Good systems keep those documents and build in controls, like prenumbered forms and monthly bank reconciliations, so nothing slips through.", 141.01], ["To find an account's balance, start with the beginning balance and net out the debits and credits. Say Cash starts at fifty thousand, gets twenty-five thousand of debits and twelve thousand of credits. The balance is sixty-three thousand.", 166.5], ["Now let's bust two myths. Myth one: credits always mean decreases. That comes from stores saying \"we'll credit your account\" when you return something. But your account is a receivable on their books, an asset, so yes, a credit reduces it. Myth two: credits always mean increases. That comes from banks. When you deposit money, the bank owes it to you, so it's a liability for the bank, and liabilities increase with credits. The truth? Whether a credit increases or decreases depends entirely on the type of account.", 182.17], ["So, the takeaways. Each account tracks one item. Debits are left, credits are right, and they always balance. DEAD and RELIC tell you which way is up. Practice those, and you're speaking accounting. See you next lesson.", 220.0]]},
  "2-2": {"src": "talks/audio/2-2.mp3?v=e8567840", "dur": 216.16, "voice": "Rob", "paras": [["Now that you know debits and credits, where do we actually write them down? In the journal.", 0.0], ["Think of a ship captain's logbook. Everything that happens gets written down, in order, by date. An accounting journal is the same idea. It's a chronological list of every transaction a company makes. But instead of long stories, each entry uses shorthand: which accounts are involved, and whether each one is debited or credited.", 5.35], ["The general journal is called the book of original entry. Here's why. A transaction happens, someone looks at the source document, figures out the debits and credits, and this is the very first place it gets recorded. You can't make financial statements from the journal alone, but it's where everything starts.", 26.88], ["Every entry follows the same layout. The date. Then the debited accounts, listed first and flush left. Then the credited accounts, indented to the right. And finally a short explanation of what happened. Debits first, credits indented, explanation last. Always.", 47.91], ["Let's watch a company called Xao Corporation in its first month. On day one, investors put money into the business in exchange for stock. Cash goes up, so we debit Cash. Equity goes up, so we credit Capital Stock. Next, Xao pays for some advertising. Advertising expense goes up, so it's debited. Cash goes down, so it's credited. See how DEAD and RELIC from last time do all the work?", 64.4], ["Now, a transaction doesn't have to involve just two accounts. On January twenty-eighth, Xao buys land for thirty thousand dollars. It pays five thousand in cash and signs a note promising to pay the other twenty-five thousand later. So the entry has one debit, Land for thirty thousand, and two credits: Cash for five thousand and Notes Payable for twenty-five thousand. Total debits, thirty thousand. Total credits, five plus twenty-five, also thirty thousand. That's called a compound entry. More accounts, same rule: debits equal credits.", 91.3], ["Real businesses can have thousands of transactions, many exactly alike. So some use special journals for the repetitive stuff. A cash receipts journal for every dollar coming in. A cash payments journal for every dollar going out. A sales journal for sales on credit, a purchases journal for purchases on credit, and a payroll journal for paychecks. The general journal is still used for everything else. Special journals just clear out the clutter.", 127.19], ["Another nice detail: journal pages are numbered. That creates a trail. If someone questions a number on the financial statements, you can trace it back to the exact page and the exact entry behind it. Some companies give every transaction its own index number for the same reason.", 157.5], ["But here's the limitation. Suppose I ask you: how much cash does Xao have at the end of January? With just a journal, you'd have to hunt through every page, find every debit and credit to Cash, and add them up. Tedious, and really easy to mess up. Now imagine hundreds of pages. We need a better tool, and that's the general ledger, coming up next.", 174.52], ["So, the recap. The journal is the book of original entry, every transaction in date order. Debits first, credits indented, and an explanation. Compound entries can have several accounts, but debits always equal credits. Great work. See you in the next one.", 199.19]]},
  "2-3": {"src": "talks/audio/2-3.mp3?v=4ab0b725", "dur": 174.47, "voice": "Rob", "paras": [["Last time we ended with a problem. The journal records everything in date order, but if you want to know how much cash a company has, you'd have to dig through every page. Today we fix that, with the general ledger.", 0.0], ["Picture two notebooks. The first one is the journal: transactions, one after another, by date. The second notebook is the ledger. It has one page for every account the company uses. A page for Cash. A page for Accounts Receivable. A page for Land, for Capital Stock, for each kind of revenue and expense. Xao Corporation used nine accounts in January, so its ledger has nine pages.", 13.91], ["So how do the amounts get from the first notebook to the second? That's called posting. To post means to copy each debit and credit from the journal onto the matching ledger page. Say the journal shows a debit to Cash and a credit to Capital Stock. You go to the Cash page and write the amount in the debit column. Then you go to the Capital Stock page and write it in the credit column. And each ledger page keeps a running balance, so after every posting you know exactly where that account stands.", 39.2], ["Now, two small habits make this system trustworthy. First, in the ledger, each line notes which journal page the amount came from. That keeps the ledger short, and it means anyone can trace any number back to the original entry. Second, in the journal, you put a check mark next to each entry once it's been posted. Why does that matter? Because in a manual system, it would be really easy to skip an entry, or post the same one twice. The check marks tell you exactly where you left off.", 69.17], ["Together, those cross-references create what's called an audit trail. You can start from a number on a financial statement, follow it into the ledger, then back to the journal, and all the way to the source document. Auditors love that. And honestly, so will you, the first time you need to find a mistake.", 98.71], ["So, remember last lesson, when we asked how much cash Xao had at the end of January? We had to hunt through the whole journal. Now, just flip to the Cash page in the ledger. There it is, twenty-six thousand three hundred dollars. Same answer, no hunting.", 121.24], ["Let's put the steps together so far. Step one: analyze each transaction. Which accounts are affected, and are they going up or down? Step two: journalize it, writing the debits and credits in the journal. Step three: periodically post those entries to the ledger. Analyze, journalize, post. That's the rhythm of accounting.", 134.72], ["So, quick recap. The general ledger has one page per account, each with a running balance. Posting copies journal amounts into the ledger. Page references and check marks create an audit trail, so nothing gets missed or doubled. And now we always know every account's balance. Nicely done. See you next time.", 155.09]]},
  "2-4": {"src": "talks/audio/2-4.mp3?v=2416b209", "dur": 207.1, "voice": "Rob", "paras": [["So far we've analyzed transactions, written them in the journal, and posted them to the ledger. Today we do a quick self-check, called the trial balance.", 0.0], ["A trial balance is simply a list of every account in the ledger, with its balance. Accounts with debit balances, like assets and expenses, go in the debit column. Accounts with credit balances, like liabilities, equity, and revenue, go in the credit column. Then you add up both columns. Since every journal entry had equal debits and credits, the two totals should match. For Xao, both columns come to sixty-one thousand three hundred dollars. Balanced.", 10.02], ["Keep in mind, the trial balance isn't a financial statement. It's an internal check. If the totals don't match, you know there's an error somewhere, and you have to find it. Trust me, it's much easier to be careful as you go than to hunt for a mistake later.", 40.75], ["But here's the catch. A balanced trial balance does not prove everything is right. It only proves debits equal credits. Plenty of mistakes keep them equal. If you forget to record a transaction entirely, both sides are missing the same amount, so it still balances. If you record the same transaction twice, both sides get doubled, still balanced. And if you post an amount to the wrong account, say Rent Expense instead of Utilities Expense, the totals don't change at all. So a balanced trial balance is a good sign, but it's not a guarantee.", 56.75], ["The trial balance is also a handy springboard to the financial statements. Revenues and expenses go to the income statement. For Xao, revenue was eleven thousand and expenses were two thousand eight hundred, so net income was eight thousand two hundred. That flows into retained earnings, and the assets, liabilities, and equity go onto the balance sheet. In the next chapter, we'll add adjustments that make these statements fully up to date. For now, think: ledger, then trial balance, then statements.", 92.36], ["Next, let's get organized. A chart of accounts lists every account the company uses, usually with a number. It's a lot like zip codes for mail. A common pattern: accounts starting with one are assets, two are liabilities, three are equity, four are revenues, and five are expenses. Many systems let you type the number instead of the name. And numbers can be split further. If Accounts Receivable is one-oh-two, each customer could get their own sub-number.", 124.82], ["That leads to our last idea: control accounts and subsidiary accounts. Say customers owe a company nineteen thousand dollars in total, spread across three customers. The company needs the total, for the balance sheet, and it also needs to know what each customer owes, to send them bills. So it keeps a separate subsidiary account for each customer. The Accounts Receivable account in the general ledger is the control account, and its balance must always equal the total of all the customer accounts. Computers are great at keeping those in sync.", 155.42], ["So, to wrap up. The trial balance checks that debits equal credits, but it can't catch every error. Statements flow from it. A chart of accounts numbers everything. And a control account always equals the sum of its subsidiary accounts. Good work today.", 191.36]]},
  "2-5": {"src": "talks/audio/2-5.mp3?v=15383eae", "dur": 178.39, "voice": "Rob", "paras": [["Let's step back and look at everything we've done this chapter, because there's an interesting pattern hiding in it.", 0.0], ["We analyzed a transaction and wrote the journal entry. Then we posted to the ledger, built a trial balance, and prepared financial statements. But think about which of those steps actually required thinking. Really, just the first one. Deciding which accounts are affected, and whether to debit or credit them, takes judgment. Everything after that is mechanical. Copying, adding, sorting, totaling.", 5.97], ["And mechanical work is exactly what computers are great at. That's why transaction processing was one of the very first business tasks ever computerized. Today, almost no one keeps their books in paper notebooks.", 31.71], ["The software comes in every size. A small business can buy a basic package for under a hundred dollars. A big organization might install an enterprise-wide system that costs millions, including the consultants to set it up. And more and more systems are cloud-based, where you enter data and run reports right over the internet.", 44.6], ["So what does using one look like? The data entry screen looks surprisingly familiar. There's a date, the accounts, debit and credit columns, and a memo line for the explanation. In other words, a journal entry. Once you save it, the software posts it to the ledger automatically, updates the trial balance, and refreshes the financial statements. And here's a nice safety feature: the software won't let you save an entry unless the debits equal the credits.", 65.81], ["Most accounting software shares a few common features. Simplified data entry screens. Separate modules for things like sales, purchases, payroll, and inventory. Pick lists and auto-complete, so you choose accounts instead of typing them. The ability to search and query the data. Information that's always up to date. And lots and lots of reports, at the push of a button.", 93.91], ["Now, you might be thinking: if the computer does all that, why am I learning debits and credits? Great question. Here's the answer, and it's an old saying in tech: garbage in, garbage out. The computer only handles the mechanical part. A person still decides what goes into that first journal entry. If that person doesn't understand accounts and debits and credits, they'll make bad choices at data entry, and every report the system produces will be wrong. Confidently, beautifully formatted, and wrong.", 115.02], ["So the knowledge you're building is what makes the software useful. You need it to set up a system, use it correctly, and make sense of the reports it spits out.", 150.53], ["So, the takeaways. After the first journal entry, accounting is mechanical, which is why computers took over. Software ranges from cheap small-business tools to huge enterprise and cloud systems. And no matter how good the software is, it needs someone who understands accounting at the keyboard. That's you. See you next time.", 158.44]]},
  "2-6": {"src": "talks/audio/2-6.mp3?v=36343a7b", "dur": 145.74, "voice": "Rob", "paras": [["This is the last lesson of chapter two, and it's a fun one: a simple tool you'll use constantly, the T-account.", 0.0], ["Grab a piece of paper and draw a big capital letter T. Write an account name across the top. Now, the left side is for debits, and the right side is for credits. That's a T-account. That's the whole thing.", 6.61], ["It's not how real businesses keep their books. It's a scratch pad. Accountants sketch T-accounts to think through a few transactions quickly, or to explain something to a colleague, or, honestly, to figure out a tricky homework problem.", 20.98], ["To find the balance, add up each side. Whichever side is bigger, the balance is the difference, on that side.", 36.41], ["Let's try it with Xao Corporation's Cash account. On the left, all the debits, money coming in, total thirty-three thousand eight hundred dollars. On the right, all the credits, money going out, total seven thousand five hundred. The debits are bigger, so Cash has a debit balance of thirty-three thousand eight hundred minus seven thousand five hundred. That's twenty-six thousand three hundred. And look: it's exactly the same balance we found in the ledger account. Same information, just a faster, more visual layout.", 43.32], ["Here's why I love T-accounts. Lay out one for every account a company uses, and you can see a whole month on one page. Every debit, every credit, and every balance, at a glance. If you do that for Xao's January, the balances match the trial balance perfectly.", 75.3], ["A tip for practicing. When you get a transaction, sketch the T-accounts for the accounts involved. Put the debit on the left of one, the credit on the right of the other, and check that they're equal. And remember DEAD and RELIC from lesson one of this chapter: assets, expenses, and dividends grow on the left; revenues, equity, and liabilities grow on the right. With those two words and a few T's on scratch paper, you can solve a surprising number of accounting problems.", 92.35], ["So, the takeaways. A T-account has debits on the left and credits on the right. It's a thinking tool, not a bookkeeping system. And its balance is the bigger side minus the smaller side, which always matches the ledger. That wraps up chapter two. You now know how transactions travel from source document to journal, to ledger, to trial balance, to statements. That's a real accomplishment. Great work.", 119.54]]},
  "3-1": {"src": "talks/audio/3-1.mp3?v=c63d81d7", "dur": 171.81, "voice": "Rob", "paras": [["Welcome to chapter three. This chapter is all about one big question: how do we measure income? And it turns out, it depends on who you ask.", 0.0], ["Ask an economist, and income is any increase in how well off you are. Your command over goods and services. Say you bought land years ago, and it's now worth a lot more. To an economist, you're richer, so you've earned income, even though you haven't sold anything.", 10.45], ["Ask an accountant, and the answer is more cautious. Accounting income focuses on transactions with other parties, recorded at historical cost. That land stays on the books at what you paid, until you actually sell it. Why so cautious? Because a sale gives us a real, verifiable number. An estimate of what the land might be worth is just an opinion. That said, accounting rules have slowly started allowing fair value for certain items, and the debate about when to record changes in value is still going.", 26.88], ["So accounting income is built from four pieces. Let's go through them.", 59.06], ["Revenues are the inflows a company gets from its central, ongoing operations. Delivering the goods and services it's in business to provide. A bakery selling bread, that's revenue.", 63.51], ["Expenses are the outflows and obligations from producing those goods and services. The bakery's flour, wages, and rent.", 74.75], ["Gains are like revenues, but they come from peripheral activities, things outside the main line of business. If the bakery sells an old delivery van for more than its book value, that's a gain, not revenue. Selling vans isn't what a bakery does.", 82.34], ["And losses are like expenses, but again from peripheral events. If the bakery sells that van for less than its book value, that's a loss.", 97.17], ["Put them together and you get the income formula: revenues plus gains, minus expenses and losses, equals income.", 108.95], ["Now, most of the time, an accounting measurement is triggered by an exchange. Someone buys, someone sells. But not always. Some events have to be recorded even when nothing is exchanged. Picture an uninsured building destroyed by a fire or a storm. Nobody bought or sold anything. But the asset is gone. So the accountant records an immediate loss: debit a loss, and credit the building to take it off the books. If we ignored it, the balance sheet would show a building that no longer exists.", 114.39], ["So, the takeaways. Economists count any increase in wealth as income; accountants mostly wait for an exchange, at historical cost. Revenues and expenses come from the main business; gains and losses come from the side. Income is revenues plus gains, minus expenses and losses. And some events, like a fire, get recorded even with no exchange. See you next lesson.", 147.09]]},
  "3-2": {"src": "talks/audio/3-2.mp3?v=dacbfa20", "dur": 183.98, "voice": "Rob", "paras": [["Here's a problem every business faces. Business never stops. Sales happen on Tuesday night, bills arrive on Sunday, customers pay whenever they feel like it. But to measure how a company is doing, we have to draw a line somewhere and say: this is the end of the period.", 0.0], ["That idea has a name: the periodicity assumption. It says business activity can be divided into measurement intervals, like months, quarters, and years.", 18.62], ["A company's year can follow the calendar, January first to December thirty-first, usually split into four quarters. Or it can use a fiscal year, which is any twelve-month period. Fiscal years often follow the natural rhythm of the business. Take a retailer. The holiday rush is in December, and then the returns pour in during January. So a retailer might end its year on January thirty-first, keeping the sales and the returns together in the same year.", 29.05], ["Here's a naming quirk to remember. A fiscal year is named for the year in which it ends. So a year running from February first, twenty X seven, through January thirty-first, twenty X eight, is called fiscal twenty X eight.", 58.83], ["Now, here's the catch. The shorter the period, the harder the measurement. Think about it. If you only reported once in a company's whole lifetime, every transaction would fall neatly inside it. But with monthly reports, lots of transactions cross the line. Insurance paid for a whole year. A project that runs for three months. Equipment that lasts for ten years. All of those have to be split across periods.", 73.04], ["So why not just wait until things settle down? Two reasons. First, you might wait a very long time before activities wrap up and become measurable with certainty. And second, investors and creditors can't wait years to find out how a business is doing. Information has to be timely to be useful. So we accept the difficulty in exchange for relevance.", 98.94], ["Once the period is set, accountants use accrual-basis accounting to assign revenues and expenses to it. Accrual accounting records revenues when they're earned and expenses when they're incurred, not just when cash changes hands. The word accrue means to come about naturally, to grow over time. That's exactly the idea: revenue builds as you earn it, regardless of when you get paid.", 121.7], ["Getting this assignment right really matters, because it determines reported income, which is what investors and lenders watch most closely. The next two lessons give the basic rules: when to recognize revenue, and when to recognize expenses.", 145.7], ["So, the recap. The periodicity assumption chops continuous business activity into months, quarters, and years. A fiscal year is any twelve months, named for the year it ends. Shorter periods mean more transactions to split. And accrual accounting puts revenues where they're earned and expenses where they're incurred. Nice work. See you next time.", 162.27]]},
  "3-3": {"src": "talks/audio/3-3.mp3?v=83b26d3b", "dur": 202.34, "voice": "Rob", "paras": [["Today's question sounds simple: when has a company actually earned its revenue? It matters a lot, because many famous accounting scandals came down to recording revenue too early.", 0.0], ["First, a bit of vocabulary. To recognize something means to record it in the accounting records. So, when do we recognize revenue? Normally, when services are performed, or when goods are sold and delivered. And two conditions both have to be met.", 11.67], ["One: there's an exchange. Goods or services change hands with a customer.", 28.33], ["Two: the earnings process is complete. The company has done what it promised. It performed the service, or it delivered the goods.", 33.62], ["Let's test that with two situations that trip people up. A car rolls off the assembly line, finished. Revenue? Not yet. Nobody bought it, so there's no exchange. Now flip it around. A customer places an order for a car that hasn't been built. Revenue? Still no. There's an agreement, but the company hasn't delivered anything, so the earnings process isn't complete. Revenue waits until both conditions are true.", 42.22], ["Now, modern business makes this harder. Think about all the ways deals get structured today. Bundles, like a phone sold with a service plan. Intangibles, like a software license. Order routing, where an online store forwards your order straight to the manufacturer to ship. And milestones, where a company gets paid only when certain research results are reached. To handle all this consistently, standard setters created a five-step model.", 70.92], ["Step one: identify the contract with the customer. Step two: identify the performance obligations, meaning each distinct thing promised. Step three: determine the transaction price, what the customer will pay. Step four: allocate that price among the obligations. And step five: recognize revenue as each obligation is satisfied.", 98.47], ["Let's walk through it with a phone bundle. A carrier sells you a phone plus a twelve-month service plan, all for six hundred dollars. If sold separately, the phone would be three hundred and the plan five hundred.", 122.39], ["The contract: that's the deal you signed. The obligations: two of them. Hand over the phone, and provide twelve months of service. The price: six hundred dollars. Now allocate it, based on the stand-alone prices, which add up to eight hundred. The phone gets three hundred over eight hundred of the six hundred dollars. That's two hundred twenty-five. The service gets five hundred over eight hundred, which is three hundred seventy-five.", 134.79], ["And finally, recognize. The phone is delivered the moment you walk out of the store, so two hundred twenty-five dollars is revenue right away. The service is delivered over time, so the three hundred seventy-five is spread over twelve months, about thirty-one dollars and twenty-five cents each month.", 163.69], ["So, to wrap up. Recognize means record. Revenue needs an exchange and a completed earnings process. Finishing production isn't enough, and neither is taking an order. And for complicated deals, follow the five steps: contract, obligations, price, allocate, recognize. Great job today.", 184.38]]},
  "3-4": {"src": "talks/audio/3-4.mp3?v=5c4e769c", "dur": 173.32, "voice": "Rob", "paras": [["Last time we figured out when to record revenue. Today, the other half: when does a cost become an expense?", 0.0], ["Here's the big idea. Accountants use one of three approaches, depending on the kind of cost.", 8.15], ["Approach one: cause and effect. Some costs are directly tied to specific revenue, so we record them in the same period as that revenue. This is called the matching principle. Think of a salesperson's commission. The commission exists because of a sale, so the commission expense is matched with that sale's revenue. Same with cost of goods sold. When a store sells a shirt, the cost of that shirt becomes an expense at the same time as the sale.", 14.24], ["Approach two: systematic and rational allocation. Some costs help a business for many years, but you can't point to any single sale they caused. Take a delivery truck. It might last eight years and help with thousands of deliveries. So instead of expensing it all at once, we spread its cost over the years it's used. That process is called depreciation, and you'll learn a lot more about it in chapter ten. For now, just picture a truck that costs forty thousand dollars and lasts eight years. Spreading it evenly, that's five thousand dollars of expense each year.", 43.3], ["Approach three: immediate recognition. Some costs have no link to revenue, and no future benefit. So we expense them right away. Severance pay for a laid-off employee is a good example. It doesn't produce any future revenue, so there's nothing to match it to and nothing to spread it over. It's an expense now.", 79.7], ["Now, here's the part that confuses a lot of people. When does cash change hands? Honestly, it doesn't matter for recognition. Revenue is recognized at the point of sale, whether the customer pays cash or promises to pay later. And expenses follow one of the three approaches, no matter when the bill actually gets paid.", 101.1], ["Why insist on that? Because most business is done on credit. If income simply followed cash in and cash out, it could give a really misleading picture. Imagine a company that makes a huge sale in December but doesn't collect until February. A cash-based report would make December look terrible and February look amazing. Accrual accounting puts the revenue in December, where it was actually earned.", 121.65], ["So, the recap. Three ways to recognize expenses. Cause and effect, the matching principle, for costs like commissions and cost of goods sold. Systematic and rational allocation, for costs that help many periods, like depreciation. And immediate recognition, for costs with no future benefit, like severance. And cash timing doesn't decide any of it. See you next lesson.", 149.04]]},
  "3-5": {"src": "talks/audio/3-5.mp3?v=32c6d5da", "dur": 215.12, "voice": "Rob", "paras": [["This is one of the most important lessons in the whole course, so settle in. It's about adjusting entries.", 0.0], ["Here's the problem. Back in chapter two, we built statements straight from the trial balance, with a warning that adjustments might be needed. Why? Because time changes account balances, even when no transaction happens. Insurance gets used up day by day. Equipment wears out. Employees earn wages every hour. Nobody writes a journal entry for each of those moments. So at the end of every period, before we prepare statements, we catch the accounts up. Think of it like briefly shutting off a pipeline to measure what's inside.", 7.3], ["Adjustments come in two families. Multi-period items, which relate to more than one period. And accruals, things earned or incurred this period but not yet recorded. Let's walk through the main ones.", 39.66], ["First, prepaid expenses. Say a company buys a three-year insurance policy on January first for nine thousand dollars. At first, that's an asset: prepaid insurance. By December thirty-first, one-third has expired. So we debit Insurance Expense three thousand and credit Prepaid Insurance three thousand. The income statement shows three thousand of expense, and the balance sheet still shows six thousand of prepaid insurance for the next two years.", 52.36], ["Supplies work the same way, with a twist. You count what's left on the shelf, and back into the amount used. Whatever you had available, minus what's left, must have been used.", 80.35], ["Next, depreciation. A truck that cost one hundred fifty thousand dollars and lasts three years depreciates fifty thousand a year, using straight-line. We debit Depreciation Expense. But here's the key detail. We don't credit the Truck account. We credit Accumulated Depreciation, which is a contra asset. It sits right under the truck and gets subtracted from it. Cost minus accumulated depreciation is the truck's book value.", 91.07], ["Then unearned revenue. Sometimes a company collects cash before it delivers, like a magazine selling a three-year subscription. That cash creates a liability, Unearned Revenue, because the company still owes the magazines. As issues are delivered, we debit Unearned Revenue and credit Revenue for the part that's been earned.", 118.95], ["Now the accruals. Accrued salaries: employees earn a thousand dollars a day, the last payday was Friday, December twenty-sixth, and they work three more days before year end. So we record three thousand dollars of salaries expense and a salaries payable, even though payday isn't until January.", 139.82], ["Accrued interest works the same way. Borrow one hundred thousand dollars at six percent for eighteen months, and you'll owe nine thousand in interest. Even if it's all paid at the end, each period records its share as time passes. Interest equals principal, times rate, times time.", 159.21], ["And accrued revenue is the mirror image. An accounting firm does work in December but bills it in January. The revenue belongs in December, so we debit Accounts Receivable and credit Revenue.", 174.92], ["After all the adjustments, we prepare an adjusted trial balance, and the real financial statements come from that.", 187.0], ["So, the big picture. Adjust at the end of every period. Prepaids and supplies get used up. Depreciation goes to a contra asset. Unearned revenue turns into revenue as it's earned. And accruals catch up what's been earned or incurred but not recorded. Get these right, and your income is right. Great work today.", 195.72]]},
  "3-6": {"src": "talks/audio/3-6.mp3?v=c32ef1b1", "dur": 186.51, "voice": "Rob", "paras": [["Let's finish chapter three with a story that shows why everything we've been learning actually matters.", 0.0], ["There are two main ways to keep score. The accrual basis records revenue when it's earned and expenses when they're incurred. That's what GAAP requires, and almost every large company uses it. The cash basis records revenue when cash comes in and expenses when cash goes out, no matter when the work happened. It's simple. But it can be really misleading in the short run, and it isn't GAAP. There's also a middle ground, the modified cash basis, which is cash basis except that big long-lived assets get recorded as assets and depreciated.", 6.11], ["Small businesses without outside investors or lenders don't have to follow GAAP, so some of them use the cash basis. Meet Ortiz Company. Ortiz designs websites and keeps its books like a checkbook register. In April, its cash went up by fifteen thousand seven hundred thirty-two dollars. On the cash basis, that's April's net income. Looks like a great month.", 40.93], ["Now here's the twist. A much bigger firm, Mega Impressions, offers to buy Ortiz for one hundred times its monthly net income, measured under GAAP. On the cash basis, one hundred times fifteen thousand seven hundred would be over one-and-a-half million dollars. Ortiz is thrilled. So an accounting firm prepares April's results on the accrual basis.", 63.81], ["Let's look at the revenue. Ortiz deposited nine thousand dollars in April, but it was an advance for work spread equally over April, May, and June. Only one-third, three thousand, was earned in April. Another deposit, about eleven thousand eight hundred dollars, collected payment for work done back in January and February. That's not April revenue at all. On the other hand, Ortiz did two thousand dollars of work in April that hasn't been collected yet. That is April revenue. Add a small job done and paid in April, and accrual revenue comes to six thousand two hundred fifty dollars.", 86.78], ["Expenses get the same treatment, and they come to about six thousand one hundred. So April's accrual net income is just one hundred forty-four dollars and twenty-five cents. Compare that to fifteen thousand seven hundred on the cash basis.", 122.52], ["One hundred times one hundred forty-four dollars is about fourteen thousand dollars. Ortiz was very disappointed, and turned the deal down.", 142.37], ["The lesson? Cash-basis results depend on when cash happens to move, so one month can look nothing like the real business. Converting to accrual strips out cash that belongs to other periods, and adds revenue earned but not collected and expenses incurred but not paid. That's why accrual accounting gives a far truer picture of each period.", 147.17], ["So, the takeaways. GAAP requires the accrual basis. The cash basis is simple but can badly distort a period. And when real money is on the line, like selling your business, the difference can be enormous. That wraps up chapter three. Really nice work.", 170.46]]},
  "4-1": {"src": "talks/audio/4-1.mp3?v=a2ab2628", "dur": 202.92, "voice": "Rob", "paras": [["Welcome to chapter four. In chapter three you learned the different kinds of adjusting entries. Now we put them to work on a real set of books, start to finish.", 0.0], ["Meet England Tours Company. It started operating early in twenty X three, and now it needs financial statements for the year ended December thirty-first. The bookkeeper has an unadjusted trial balance. If we built statements straight from that, they'd be incomplete and wrong. Insurance has been used up, equipment has worn out, salaries and other bills have built up. So first, adjustments.", 10.28], ["In practice, someone who knows the business well reviews the trial balance and lists the adjustments that are needed. And here's an important control point. Not just anyone should be able to enter year-end adjustments, or errors and even rogue entries can sneak in. So each proposed adjustment is written up on a form, often called a journal voucher, and approved by the chief accountant or controller. Those approved vouchers become the supporting documents for the entries.", 32.47], ["After the adjustments are posted, we prepare an adjusted trial balance. For England Tours, it still balances, now at sixty thousand dollars. And you'll notice some new accounts showing up: accumulated depreciation, a couple of payables, and several new expenses. Other balances have changed too.", 62.0], ["Here's the good news. The adjusted trial balance is usually all you need to prepare the financial statements. Revenues and expenses go to the income statement. Net income flows into the statement of retained earnings, along with dividends. And the assets, liabilities, and equity go onto the balance sheet. You can trace every number straight from the adjusted trial balance into the statements.", 80.56], ["Now, you might say: my software does all of that automatically. True! Once the adjustments are entered, any accounting package races through the rest. But that's a bit like saying you don't need to understand addition because you own a calculator. You still need to understand how the numbers flow, so you can spot when something's off.", 102.92], ["Finally, the worksheet. Sometimes you want adjusted statements without actually changing the journal and ledger. Maybe a manager wants monthly reports, but the company only books adjustments once a year. Or an auditor wants to see the effect of proposed adjustments first. A worksheet handles that. It's a big spreadsheet, filled in one pair of columns at a time: the trial balance, the adjustments, the adjusted trial balance, and then columns for the income statement, retained earnings, and the balance sheet.", 123.96], ["Each adjusted balance moves into exactly one pair of columns, on the same side. Cash, a debit, goes to the balance sheet debit column. Revenue, a credit, goes to the income statement credit column. For England Tours, the income statement credits are thirty-two thousand eight hundred and the debits are thirty thousand two hundred, so net income is two thousand six hundred. That net income moves over into the retained earnings columns. And after dividends of one thousand, retained earnings ends at one thousand six hundred, which carries to the balance sheet.", 157.93], ["So, the recap. Trial balance, then approved adjusting entries, then the adjusted trial balance, then the statements. And a worksheet lets you see adjusted statements without touching the books. Nice work. See you next lesson.", 189.45]]},
  "4-2": {"src": "talks/audio/4-2.mp3?v=45449ee1", "dur": 218.46, "voice": "Rob", "paras": [["Over the last few chapters, you've learned a whole series of steps. Today, we put them together into one big loop, called the accounting cycle, and add the very last step: closing the books.", 0.0], ["Here's the cycle. Journalize transactions. Post them to the ledger. Prepare a trial balance. Make adjusting entries. Prepare an adjusted trial balance. Prepare the financial statements. Close the books. And prepare a post-closing trial balance. Then, the next period, it all starts again. It captures every transaction, moves it in an orderly way into useful statements, and leaves a complete record behind. No wonder it's lasted for centuries.", 12.99], ["So why do we need to close? Closing has two goals.", 44.14], ["Goal one: update Retained Earnings. Here's something sneaky. When we record revenues, expenses, and dividends during the year, we never actually touch the Retained Earnings account. So until we close, the ledger still shows the beginning-of-year balance. Closing brings it up to date.", 46.53], ["Goal two: reset the temporary accounts. Revenues, expenses, and dividends measure just one period. Next year has to start fresh, at zero. That's why they're called temporary, or nominal, accounts. Assets, liabilities, and equity are different. They're permanent, or real, accounts, and their balances carry forward. You wouldn't start the new year pretending you have no cash.", 68.19], ["Closing takes four entries, and they use a special temporary account called Income Summary. Think of it as a funnel.", 94.67], ["Entry one: close the revenues. Debit each revenue account for its balance, and credit Income Summary. Now revenues are zero.", 102.4], ["Entry two: close the expenses. Credit each expense account, and debit Income Summary. Now expenses are zero too.", 112.02], ["At this point, Income Summary holds the period's net income. For England Tours, that's thirty-two thousand eight hundred of revenue credits, minus thirty thousand two hundred of expense debits, leaving a two thousand six hundred dollar credit.", 120.65], ["Entry three: close Income Summary to Retained Earnings. Debit Income Summary two thousand six hundred, credit Retained Earnings. Now Income Summary is zero.", 135.88], ["Entry four: close Dividends. Credit Dividends, and debit Retained Earnings.", 147.76], ["When the dust settles, every temporary account is zero, and Retained Earnings shows its updated balance. For England Tours, that's one thousand six hundred dollars. Beginning retained earnings, plus income, minus dividends, equals ending retained earnings.", 153.85], ["Last step: the post-closing trial balance. It lists the balances after closing, so it only has balance sheet accounts. It's one more check that debits still equal credits.", 171.37], ["One modern twist. Database-style accounting software can pull results for literally any time span, like the forty-five days ending October fifteenth, years ago. So in those systems, the idea of closing the books matters less than it used to. But the logic is the same.", 182.89], ["So, the recap. The accounting cycle runs from journalizing to the post-closing trial balance, every period. Closing updates Retained Earnings and zeroes out revenues, expenses, and dividends, using four entries through Income Summary. Great work today.", 201.04]]},
  "4-3": {"src": "talks/audio/4-3.mp3?v=3e9d6c5e", "dur": 156.68, "voice": "Rob", "paras": [["Today's lesson is a short one, about a handy little shortcut called the reversing entry.", 0.0], ["A reversing entry is an optional journal entry, made on the very first day of a new period. And it's the exact opposite of an adjusting entry you made at the end of the last period. Why would you ever undo something you just did? Because it can make the next routine entry much simpler. Let's see how.", 5.66], ["Here's the setup. At the end of twenty X three, employees have earned two thousand dollars of salaries that haven't been paid yet. So the adjusting entry was: debit Salaries Expense two thousand, credit Salaries Payable two thousand. Then, on the next payday, January fifteenth, twenty X four, employees get paid five thousand dollars. Two thousand of that is for work at the end of last year, and three thousand is for work this January.", 24.26], ["Option one: no reversing entry. On January fifteenth, the bookkeeper has to remember that accrual from last year and split the payment. Debit Salaries Payable two thousand, debit Salaries Expense three thousand, and credit Cash five thousand. That works, but it means somebody has to remember. And in a busy office with lots of accruals, people forget.", 50.96], ["Option two: use a reversing entry. On January first, you flip last year's adjustment: debit Salaries Payable two thousand, and credit Salaries Expense two thousand. Then, on January fifteenth, payroll is completely normal. Debit Salaries Expense five thousand, credit Cash five thousand. No remembering, no splitting.", 73.97], ["Now, that January first credit to an expense account looks strange on its own. An expense with a credit balance? But it only makes sense paired with the January fifteenth debit. Five thousand debit, minus two thousand credit, leaves three thousand of salary expense for twenty X four, which is exactly right. And Salaries Payable ends at zero either way.", 95.99], ["So which adjustments are worth reversing? Ordinarily, the ones that record accrued revenues and accrued expenses, specifically when there's a future cash flow coming. Accrued salaries, accrued interest, revenue you'll collect later. Those are the ones where a routine cash entry is coming, and reversing lets you record it normally.", 117.74], ["So, the takeaways. A reversing entry is the exact opposite of an adjusting entry, made on the first day of the new period. It's optional. It lets the next routine entry be recorded without special attention. And with or without it, the final balances come out exactly the same. See you next lesson.", 137.47]]},
  "4-4": {"src": "talks/audio/4-4.mp3?v=a97c8145", "dur": 191.09, "voice": "Rob", "paras": [["Today we're going to make the balance sheet a lot more useful, by organizing it. The result is called a classified balance sheet.", 0.0], ["Quick reminder first. Not everything on a balance sheet is at current value. Some assets are at historical cost, and some valuable things, like a brand name, aren't reported at all. Even so, the balance sheet is essential for judging a company's financial health. And grouping the accounts into meaningful categories, with subtotals, makes that analysis much easier.", 7.89], ["Let's start with assets. There can be up to five sections.", 29.9], ["Current assets come first. That's cash, and things that will turn into cash or be used up soon: short-term investments, receivables, inventory, and prepaid expenses. They're listed in order of liquidity, most liquid first. And here's the key definition. \"Current\" means within one year, or within the operating cycle, whichever is longer. The operating cycle is the time it takes to turn cash back into cash: buy inventory, sell it on account, and collect the receivable. For most businesses, that's less than a year.", 33.69], ["Next come long-term investments, like stock in another company held for the long haul. Then property, plant, and equipment, the land, buildings, and machinery the business uses. Then intangible assets, things like patents and trademarks that have no physical form. And finally, other assets, for anything that doesn't fit elsewhere.", 65.81], ["Liabilities get split too. Current liabilities are due within one year or the operating cycle, and are normally paid from current assets. Long-term liabilities are everything else, like bank loans and mortgage notes. Here's a detail that trips people up. If a company has a long-term note, the part of the principal that's due next year is classified as current. The rest stays long-term.", 87.89], ["Then equity. For a corporation, that's capital stock, what investors paid for their shares, plus retained earnings, the profits plowed back into the business. A sole proprietorship just has one capital account for the owner. A partnership has one capital account for each partner. Newer business forms, like L L Cs, change the labels, but not the overall structure.", 111.68], ["Put it all together for Classy Company, and you get a balance sheet where you can see at a glance what's short-term, what's long-term, and who has claims on what.", 132.97], ["Last idea, and it's a big one. Notes to the financial statements. Many important facts can't be expressed as a dollar amount: accounting policies, major events, pending lawsuits. The full disclosure principle requires that the statements and their notes include every fact that would influence an investor's or creditor's judgment. Honestly, the notes are often longer than the statements themselves.", 146.71], ["So, the recap. Asset sections: current assets, long-term investments, property, plant, and equipment, intangibles, and other assets. Current means within a year or the operating cycle, whichever is longer. Liabilities are current or long-term. And full disclosure means the notes tell the rest of the story. Great work today.", 171.03]]},
  "4-5": {"src": "talks/audio/4-5.mp3?v=78bb268b", "dur": 183.25, "voice": "Rob", "paras": [["Let's finish chapter four with a practical question every lender and investor asks: can this company pay its bills as they come due? That ability is called liquidity.", 0.0], ["Liquidity really matters. A company can have great long-term prospects and tons of valuable assets, and still go under if it can't pay this month's bills.", 10.86], ["The simplest measure is working capital: current assets minus current liabilities. Take Classy Company, from last lesson. It has four hundred fifty thousand dollars of current assets and one hundred fifty thousand of current liabilities. So working capital is three hundred thousand. You'd hope to see a positive number. A negative one can be a sign of financial stress.", 20.93], ["But be careful, because negative working capital isn't always bad. A company might have a standby line of credit at the bank. Or think about a fast-food burger chain. Customers pay in cash, so there are almost no receivables. Bread and milk don't keep, so there's very little inventory. But there are sizable bills owed to suppliers. On paper, working capital might be negative. Yet the business thrives, because cash flows through it so fast, at good margins, that it easily pays its bills and even funds new restaurants.", 43.58], ["Here's another problem with working capital. Is one million dollars a lot? For a giant corporation, it's a drop in the bucket. For a family business, it could be plenty. So we use ratios, which put the pieces in proportion.", 75.9], ["The current ratio is current assets divided by current liabilities. For Classy, four hundred fifty thousand divided by one hundred fifty thousand. That's three to one.", 89.54], ["The quick ratio is tougher. It only counts the most liquid assets: cash, short-term investments, and receivables. It leaves out inventory and prepaid expenses. For Classy, that's one hundred thousand of cash, fifty thousand of investments, and seventy-five thousand of receivables, which is two hundred twenty-five thousand, divided by one hundred fifty thousand. That's one and a half to one.", 101.84], ["Why bother with the quick ratio? Because a company could be sitting on a mountain of inventory that just won't sell. Its current ratio looks great, but it can't actually pay its bills. The quick ratio catches that.", 124.4], ["One more warning. Ratios can be manipulated. Here's a classic trick. Use cash to pay off some bills right before year end. Both current assets and current liabilities drop by the same amount, and the current ratio actually goes up. Nothing about the business got better, but the ratio looks better. So never draw a blanket conclusion from any single number.", 140.58], ["So, the takeaways. Liquidity is the ability to pay near-term bills. Working capital is current assets minus current liabilities. The current ratio divides the two. The quick ratio is a stricter test, leaving out inventory and prepaids. And always look behind the ratios. That wraps up chapter four. Nice job.", 162.38]]},
  "5-1": {"src": "talks/audio/5-1.mp3?v=bd30f5eb", "dur": 208.77, "voice": "Rob", "paras": [["Welcome to chapter five. So far, our examples have mostly been service businesses, like law firms and consultants. Now we turn to merchants: businesses that buy inventory and resell it for more, in stores or online.", 0.0], ["A merchant's income statement adds an important middle step called gross profit. Gross profit equals sales, minus the cost of the goods that were sold. Why does it matter? Because it tells you whether the company sells its products for comfortably more than they cost. If the gross profit margin is thin, the business can struggle to make money even as sales grow. But if it's strong, every extra sale can really boost net income, especially when operating expenses stay about the same.", 15.2], ["Let's look at how sales get recorded. When a merchant sells goods, it credits Sales, a revenue account, and debits either Cash or Accounts Receivable, depending on whether the customer paid now or later.", 44.67], ["Now, what if a customer returns something? Say a customer returns a thousand dollars of goods they bought on account. You might expect us to just reverse the sale. But we don't. Instead, we debit a special account called Sales Returns and Allowances, and credit Accounts Receivable. That account is a contra-revenue account, meaning it's subtracted from sales. Why the extra account? Because managers want to keep an eye on returns. A rising level of returns can signal unhappy customers. Sales, minus returns and allowances, gives us net sales.", 57.3], ["An allowance is a little different from a return. If an item is slightly damaged, the customer might keep it in exchange for a price reduction. That reduction is an allowance, and it uses the same accounts.", 95.73], ["Next, trade discounts. Catalogs often show a list price, but customers actually pay a lower invoice price after a trade discount. Accountants simply record the sale at the invoice price, the actual exchange price. The trade discount itself is never recorded.", 106.86], ["Credit cards. Store-issued cards encourage spending and can earn interest, but they come with costs: bad debts, a credit department, billing. Bank cards like Visa and Mastercard are simpler. The merchant gets paid almost right away, minus a fee of roughly one to three percent. Most merchants record the full sale as revenue and the card fee as an expense. So a thousand-dollar sale with a two percent fee is a thousand of revenue and twenty dollars of expense.", 123.04], ["Finally, cash discounts, which reward fast payment. You'll see terms like two ten, net thirty. That means a two percent discount if paid within ten days, otherwise the full amount is due within thirty. Say Barber Shop Supply sells a thousand dollars of goods on those terms. If the customer pays within ten days, it sends nine hundred eighty. Barber Shop debits Cash nine eighty, debits Sales Discounts twenty, and credits Accounts Receivable the full thousand, because the whole debt is settled. Sales Discounts is another contra-revenue account.", 154.71], ["So, the recap. Gross profit is sales minus cost of goods sold. Returns, allowances, and discounts go to contra-revenue accounts that reduce net sales. Trade discounts aren't recorded. Card fees are an expense. And two ten, net thirty means two percent off within ten days. See you next lesson.", 188.3]]}
};
